<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create_property_index.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-createpropertyindex">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-createpropertyindex">
  <primary>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</refname>
  <refpurpose>build an index on one or more properties of a label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE [ UNIQUE ] PROPERTY INDEX [ CONCURRENTLY ] [ [ IF NOT EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> ]
    ON [ ONLY ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> [ USING <replaceable class="parameter">method</replaceable> ]
    ( { <replaceable class="parameter">property</replaceable> | ( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ) } [ COLLATE <replaceable class="parameter">collation</replaceable> ] [ <replaceable class="parameter">opclass</replaceable> ] [ ASC | DESC ] [ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ] [, ...] )
    [ NULLS [ NOT ] DISTINCT ]
    [ WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] ) ]
    [ TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable> ]
    [ WHERE <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createpropertyindex-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   There is no separate kind of index behind this statement. A property index is an ordinary
   index over an expression that reads the property out of the label's <type>jsonb</type>
   bag. <literal>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX ON movie (title)</literal> builds a btree whose key is
   <literal>(properties.'title')</literal>, and the planner has to see the predicate in the
   same form to use it.
  </para>

  <para>
   That expression is the whole story of the statement's limits. The indexed value is of type
   <type>jsonb</type>, so the access method has to have a default operator class for
   <type>jsonb</type>. Three do: <literal>btree</literal>, the default,
   <literal>hash</literal> and <literal>gin</literal>. <literal>gist</literal>,
   <literal>spgist</literal> and <literal>brin</literal> do not. For the same reason no
   collation applies to the value, and the value can never be an included column:
   <literal>INCLUDE</literal> is refused with
   <literal>expressions are not supported in included columns</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   An index on a promoted property is different, because the value is then the column, and
   the column's own type decides what is allowed. A bare key, <literal>( year )</literal>,
   indexes the promoted column. A parenthesised expression,
   <literal>( (lower(title)) )</literal>, still reads the bag, promoted key or not.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createpropertyindex-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>UNIQUE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Reject a row whose indexed value duplicates an existing one, checking the rows that are
      already there when the index is built.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Build the index without locking out writes to the label, at the cost of two table
      scans.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal></term>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name for the index. Left out, a name is derived from the label and the indexed
      properties, so <literal>movie</literal> and <literal>year</literal> give
      <literal>movie_year_idx</literal>. <literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal> issues a notice
      instead of an error when a relation of that name already exists, so it needs a name.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ONLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Accepted, and without effect. An index on a parent label covers that label's own rows
      either way, so a hierarchy needs one index for each label that will be scanned.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label whose rows are indexed, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>. It
      cannot be qualified with a graph name.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>USING</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">method</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Index access method. Over a property read the choice is <literal>btree</literal>, the
      default, <literal>hash</literal> or <literal>gin</literal>. Over a promoted column, any
      method with a default operator class for that column's type.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">property</replaceable></term>
    <term>( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> )</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The key. A property is written the way Cypher writes it: <literal>title</literal>, or
      <literal>name.first</literal> for a key inside a nested map. An expression is any
      parenthesised Cypher expression over the label's properties, for example
      <literal>(year / 10)</literal>. See <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>COLLATE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">collation</replaceable></term>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">opclass</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Collation and operator class for the indexed value, for example
      <literal>jsonb_path_ops</literal> for a gin index. A collation is usable only over a
      promoted column of a collatable type, since a property read fails with
      <literal>collations are not supported by type jsonb</literal>. Operator class
      parameters are not part of the statement.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ASC</literal> | <literal>DESC</literal> | <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> | <literal>NULLS LAST</literal></term>
    <term><literal>NULLS</literal> [ <literal>NOT</literal> ] <literal>DISTINCT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Sort order stored in the index. <literal>ASC</literal> and <literal>NULLS LAST</literal>
      are the defaults, and <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> is the default under
      <literal>DESC</literal>. <literal>NULLS [ NOT ] DISTINCT</literal> decides, for a unique
      index, whether two nulls count as different values.
      <literal>NULLS DISTINCT</literal>, the default, lets an index hold many nulls.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WITH</literal> ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] )</term>
    <term><literal>TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Where the index is stored, and how. The storage parameters are those of the access
      method that was chosen, such as <literal>fillfactor</literal> for btree or
      <literal>fastupdate</literal> for gin. Without a tablespace,
      <varname>default_tablespace</varname> decides.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WHERE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Build a partial index, covering only the rows the condition accepts.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createpropertyindex-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH indexdemo;
SET graph_path = indexdemo;
CREATE VLABEL movie;
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Arrival', year: 2016, genre: 'Sci-Fi'}),
       (:movie {title: 'Dune', year: 2021, genre: 'Sci-Fi'}),
       (:movie {title: 'Sicario', year: 2015, genre: 'Thriller'});
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Index a property:
<programlisting>
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_idx ON movie (title);

CREATE PROPERTY INDEX
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The planner matches an equality on <literal>title</literal> to the index expression. Three
   vertices are far too few for it to prefer an index scan, so the setting below makes the
   plan visible:
<programlisting>
SET enable_seqscan = off;
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Dune'}) RETURN m.year;

                      QUERY PLAN
------------------------------------------------------
 Index Scan using movie_title_idx on movie m
   Index Cond: (properties.'title' = '"Dune"'::jsonb)
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>gin</literal> and <literal>hash</literal> are the alternatives to btree over a
   property read. The other three access methods have no default operator class for
   <type>jsonb</type>, so they cannot index one. <literal>spgist</literal> and
   <literal>brin</literal> fail the way <literal>gist</literal> does here:
<programlisting>
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX movie_genre_gist ON movie USING gist (genre);

ERROR:  data type jsonb has no default operator class for access method "gist"
HINT:  You must specify an operator class for the index or define a default operator class for the data type.
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Reject duplicate values. The <literal>Key</literal> in the message is the index
   expression, which is how a unique property index differs from a unique column constraint:
<programlisting>
CREATE UNIQUE PROPERTY INDEX movie_title_uniq ON movie (title);
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Dune', year: 1984});

ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint "movie_title_uniq"
DETAIL:  Key ((properties.'title'))=("Dune") already exists.
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A label whose property is promoted to a column indexes the column instead, and the plan
   then compares integers rather than <type>jsonb</type>. On this label
   <literal>(name COLLATE "C")</literal>, <literal>(name text_pattern_ops)</literal> and
   <literal>USING brin (year)</literal> are accepted too. None reads the bag:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL title (year int GENERATED, name text GENERATED);
CREATE (:title {name: 'Arrival', year: 2016}), (:title {name: 'Dune', year: 2021});
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX title_year_idx ON title (year);
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) MATCH (t:title) WHERE t.year &gt; 2010 RETURN t.name;

                 QUERY PLAN
--------------------------------------------
 Index Scan using title_year_idx on title t
   Index Cond: (year &gt; 2010)
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>RESET enable_seqscan</literal> and
   <literal>DROP GRAPH indexdemo CASCADE</literal> undo what the examples did.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createpropertyindex-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no index DDL. Its catalog modifying statements reach only schemas,
   graphs and graph types, in clause 12, so this statement is an AgensGraph extension.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</command> corresponds to <command>CREATE INDEX</command> on
   an expression, and <literal>UNIQUE</literal>, <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>,
   <literal>USING</literal>, <literal>COLLATE</literal>, the operator class, the sort order,
   <literal>NULLS [ NOT ] DISTINCT</literal>, <literal>WITH</literal>,
   <literal>TABLESPACE</literal> and <literal>WHERE</literal> all mean there what they mean
   here. Three differences matter. The index target is written as a Cypher property rather
   than a column, so the parser builds the <literal>properties</literal> read for it.
   <literal>INCLUDE</literal> and operator class parameters cannot be used. And
   <xref linkend="cypher-droppropertyindex"/> takes only a property index, while
   <command>DROP INDEX</command> takes any index of a label, a property index included.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

